#!/bin/bash
set -e

if [ -f prompt.md ] || [ -f prompt-human.md ]; then
  read -p "prompt.md and/or prompt-human.md already exist. Overwrite both? [y/N] " confirm
  [[ "$confirm" != "y" && "$confirm" != "Y" ]] && exit 0
fi

PROMPT_FILE=$(mktemp)
trap 'rm -f "$PROMPT_FILE"' EXIT

cat > "$PROMPT_FILE" <<'RALPH_PROMPT_EOF'
Generate TWO files in the current working directory, both tailored to THIS repository:

1. `prompt.md` — for the autonomous `ralph` iterative-coding loop (picks `ready-for-agent` issues)
2. `prompt-human.md` — for interactive human-collaborator sessions (picks `ready-for-human` issues, surfaces decisions to the user)

## Step 1 — inspect the repo

Detect the language(s) and primary tooling. Look for: package.json, bun.lockb, go.mod, Cargo.toml, pyproject.toml, Gemfile, mix.exs, deno.json, Makefile, etc. Read the relevant manifest(s) to identify:

- The TEST command (e.g. `bun run test`, `npm test`, `go test ./...`, `cargo test`, `pytest`, `mix test`, `make -C subdir test`)
- The TYPECHECK / static-analysis / BUILD command (e.g. `bun run typecheck`, `tsc --noEmit`, `go vet ./...`, `go build ./...`, `cargo check`, `mypy`, `ruff check`, `dialyzer`, `make -C subdir build`)
- Any LINT or FORMAT command the project actually uses (only if defined in scripts/Makefile/etc — do NOT invent one)

If a category does not apply to this stack, omit that bullet entirely — never invent commands.

Also peek at `convex/`, `app/`, `src/`, `go-htmx/`, etc. so you understand whether there are extra feedback loops worth running (e.g. `bunx convex dev --once` for Convex projects). Only include such commands if the repo clearly uses them.

## Step 2 — write prompt.md (autonomous agent)

Write the file using the `<skeleton-agent>` below VERBATIM, except the `# FEEDBACK LOOPS` section, where you replace the placeholder bullets with the real commands you found in step 1.

<skeleton-agent>
# ISSUE SOURCE

Issues for this repo live as markdown files under `.scratch/<feature-slug>/issues/<NN>-<slug>.md`. Their parent PRDs are at `.scratch/<feature-slug>/PRD.md`. Each file has a `Status:` line near the top.

You have been passed a file containing the last 10 RALPH commits (SHA, date, full message). Review these to understand which issues have already been completed.

# TASK SELECTION

Scan all `.scratch/*/issues/*.md` for issues with `Status: ready-for-agent`. Order them by feature directory, then by the numeric prefix of the filename.

Skip any issue whose `Status:` line is `done` (terminal status — work already finished and the file has been flipped).

Skip any issue whose "Blocked by" reference is not yet present in the passed RALPH commit log.

Skip any issue whose own filename is already referenced in the passed RALPH commit log (it has been completed).

Pick the first remaining issue. If none remain, emit <promise>NO MORE TASKS</promise>.

# EXPLORATION

Read the chosen issue file fully. Read its parent PRD (`../PRD.md` from the issue file). Read any completed blocker issues for context. Then explore the repo and fill your context with information relevant to the task.

# EXECUTION

Complete the task as described in the "What to build" section, satisfying every checkbox in "Acceptance criteria".

Make the smallest change that satisfies the issue. Do not outrun your headlights.

If anything blocks completion, output <promise>ABORT</promise>.

# FEEDBACK LOOPS

Before committing, run the project feedback loops:

- REPLACE_WITH_TEST_COMMAND
- REPLACE_WITH_TYPECHECK_OR_BUILD_COMMAND

If any feedback loop fails, fix the issue before committing. If you cannot fix it, output <promise>ABORT</promise>.

# COMMIT

Before staging, **flip the `Status:` line on the chosen issue file from `ready-for-agent` to `done`**. Stage that change together with the rest of the work — same commit, same PR. This is the durable record that survives outside the 10-commit log window.

Make a single git commit. The commit message MUST:

1. Start with the `RALPH:` prefix
2. Reference the issue path (e.g. `.scratch/<slug>/issues/01-foo.md`)
3. State the task completed
4. List key decisions made
5. List files changed (including the issue file's `Status:` flip)
6. Note blockers or follow-ups for the next iteration

Keep it concise.

# FINAL RULES

ONLY WORK ON A SINGLE TASK PER RUN.
</skeleton-agent>

## Step 3 — write prompt-human.md (interactive human-collaborator)

Write a parallel file using the `<skeleton-human>` below VERBATIM, again replacing the `# FEEDBACK LOOPS` placeholders with the real commands from step 1.

<skeleton-human>
# ISSUE SOURCE

Issues for this repo live as markdown files under `.scratch/<feature-slug>/issues/<NN>-<slug>.md`. Their parent PRDs are at `.scratch/<feature-slug>/PRD.md`. Each file has a `Status:` line near the top.

This prompt is invoked at the **start of an interactive session** with the human user. The user is at the keyboard and will collaborate on decisions, sign-offs, and any step that requires judgement. You drive the work; the user reviews and approves.

You have been passed a file containing the last 10 RALPH commits (SHA, date, full message). Review these to understand which issues have already been completed.

# TASK SELECTION

Scan all `.scratch/*/issues/*.md` for issues with `Status: ready-for-human`. Order them by feature directory, then by the numeric prefix of the filename.

Skip any issue whose `Status:` line is `done` (terminal status — work already finished and the file has been flipped).

Skip any issue whose "Blocked by" reference is not yet present in the passed RALPH commit log.

Skip any issue whose own filename is already referenced in the passed RALPH commit log (it has been completed).

Pick the first remaining issue. If none remain, emit <promise>NO MORE TASKS</promise>.

# KICKOFF WITH THE USER

Before exploring or executing, surface the chosen issue to the user in one short message:

- Issue path
- One-sentence summary of what it builds
- Why it needs human involvement (the specific decision points, sign-offs, or interactive validations the issue calls out)
- Estimated rough scope (small / medium / large) based on the acceptance criteria

Then ask the user whether to proceed with this issue or pick a different one.

If the user redirects to a different issue, switch to that one. If the user confirms, proceed.

# EXPLORATION

Read the chosen issue file fully. Read its parent PRD (`../PRD.md` from the issue file). Read any completed blocker issues for context. Explore the repo and fill your context with information relevant to the task.

If the issue calls for an investigation phase before code (e.g., diff investigation, SPEC document, audit), produce that artifact first and **bring it to the user for sign-off before any code lands**.

# EXECUTION

Complete the task as described in the "What to build" section, satisfying every checkbox in "Acceptance criteria".

Make the smallest change that satisfies the issue. Do not outrun your headlights.

For any acceptance criterion that explicitly requires human judgement (e.g., "project owner signs off", "manual approval before merge", "user confirms visually matches"), **pause and surface the artifact to the user**. Do not self-approve human-gated criteria. The whole point of `ready-for-human` is that you and the user collaborate; do not skip the collaboration.

For decision points that emerge during implementation (architectural fork, library choice, scope ambiguity), pause and ask the user. Recommend an answer; let the user decide.

If anything blocks completion that the user cannot unblock, output <promise>ABORT</promise>.

# FEEDBACK LOOPS

Before committing, run the project feedback loops:

- REPLACE_WITH_TEST_COMMAND
- REPLACE_WITH_TYPECHECK_OR_BUILD_COMMAND

If any feedback loop fails, fix the issue before committing. If you cannot fix it, surface the failure to the user and ask how to proceed; do not silently abort.

# COMMIT

When the user confirms the work is complete (all acceptance criteria checked, all human-gated artifacts approved), **flip the `Status:` line on the chosen issue file from `ready-for-human` to `done`**. Stage that change together with the rest of the work — same commit, same PR. This is the durable record that survives outside the 10-commit log window.

Make a single git commit. The commit message MUST:

1. Start with the `RALPH:` prefix
2. Reference the issue path (e.g. `.scratch/<slug>/issues/09-foo.md`)
3. State the task completed
4. List key decisions made (especially the user-approved ones)
5. List files changed (including the issue file's `Status:` flip)
6. Note any blockers or follow-ups for the next iteration

Keep it concise.

# FINAL RULES

ONLY WORK ON A SINGLE TASK PER RUN.

Never self-approve a `ready-for-human` checkbox that names "project owner" or "manual approval" — those are explicit collaboration gates.
</skeleton-human>

## Step 4 — write both files

Write the adapted `prompt.md` AND `prompt-human.md` to the current working directory. Preserve the literal `<promise>NO MORE TASKS</promise>` and `<promise>ABORT</promise>` tags exactly as shown — the ralph runner greps for those strings.

Do not output anything else, do not modify other files, do not run tests or commits.
RALPH_PROMPT_EOF

claude --dangerously-skip-permissions --print "$(cat "$PROMPT_FILE")"

echo
echo "prompt.md and prompt-human.md generated. Review them before running ralph."
